artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a model of quantum computation in which a quantum computer is characterized by its number of available physical qubits Q and by hardware-imposed limits on gate fidelity, coherence time, and per-cycle classical/quantum processing throughput. Suppose that running Shor's algorithm to factor a 2048-bit RSA integer with reasonable error correction requires on the order of 2·10^7 physical qubits, while the largest available device satisfies Q ≤ 1121. Develop a framework for designing application-layer quantum communication protocols and identifying real-world computational tasks T with the following properties:

(1) T admits a quantum algorithm A_T whose total physical-qubit requirement, circuit depth, and number of two-qubit gate operations all fit within hardware budgets achievable with Q on the order of 10^3 qubits and current coherence-time and gate-fidelity constraints;

(2) A_T provably exploits a quantum resource (entanglement, superposition, no-cloning, contextuality, or quantum measurement statistics) in such a way that no classical algorithm or protocol can solve T with comparable performance under a precisely stated cost metric (time complexity, communication complexity, sample complexity, or security parameter);

(3) The protocol is implementable as an application-layer service over a quantum network stack whose underlying physical and link layers have specified loss, decoherence, and rate parameters.

Formally characterize the class of tasks T satisfying (1)–(3), and exhibit concrete examples of such tasks together with rigorous proofs of their quantum advantage and feasibility within the stated hardware envelope.
